########################################
#check after installing pod identity
########################################
i)kubectl get pods \
  -n kube-system \
  -l app.kubernetes.io/instance=eks-pod-identity-agent

You should expect the Pod Identity Agent pods to be running on your nodes.
For example:
NAME                                      READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
eks-pod-identity-agent-xxxxx              1/1     Running   0          2m
eks-pod-identity-agent-yyyyy              1/1     Running   0          2m

One important point
Installing the Pod Identity Agent is only the first half of using EKS Pod Identity.

Later, when you want an application such as Argo CD or your application workload to access AWS services (S3, ECR, Secrets Manager, SQS, etc.), you'll also need:

For example, the Terraform resource you'll eventually use is:

resource "aws_eks_pod_identity_association" "myapp" {
  cluster_name    = var.cluster_name
  namespace       = var.app_namespace
  service_account = "myapp"
  role_arn        = aws_iam_role.myapp.arn
}

############################################################################
#check after installing aws-loadbalancer-controller 
############################################################################
i)terraform apply

ii)kubectl get deployment \
  -n kube-system \
  aws-load-balancer-controller

You should see something similar to:
NAME                           READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE
aws-load-balancer-controller   2/2     2            2           5m

For a more useful check:

kubectl get pods \
  -n kube-system \
  -l app.kubernetes.io/name=aws-load-balancer-controller

And:
kubectl describe deployment \
  -n kube-system \
  aws-load-balancer-controller
################################################################
#And the four AWS pieces in aws-load-balancer-controller.tf are:
################################################################

IAM Policy — what the controller is allowed to do.
IAM Role — the identity the controller assumes.
Policy Attachment — connects the policy to the role.
EKS Pod Identity Association — connects the IAM role to the Kubernetes ServiceAccount.
That's the complete chain you need for the controller.



################################################################
#value.yaml
################################################################
infrastructure/
└── argocd/
    ├── argocd.tf
    └── values.yaml

is appropriate. Argo CD's server continues to speak HTTPS internally.

I'd structure the values file like this:

global:
  domain: argocd.example.com

  networkPolicy:
    create: true
    defaultDenyIngress: true

configs:
  params:
    server.insecure: "false"

server:
  replicas: 2

  ingress:
    enabled: true
    controller: aws
    ingressClassName: alb

    hostname: argocd.example.com

    # TLS is terminated by the AWS ALB.
    tls: false

    annotations:
      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/scheme: internal
      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/target-type: ip

      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/listen-ports: '[{"HTTPS":443}]'

      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/certificate-arn: "arn:aws:acm:eu-west-1:123456789012:certificate/xxxxxxxx"

      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "443"

      # ALB -> Argo CD server uses HTTPS.
      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/backend-protocol: HTTPS

      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/healthcheck-protocol: HTTPS
      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/healthcheck-path: /healthz
      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/success-codes: "200"

  resources:
    requests:
      cpu: 250m
      memory: 256Mi

    limits:
      cpu: "1"
      memory: 1Gi


repoServer:
  replicas: 2

  resources:
    requests:
      cpu: 250m
      memory: 256Mi

    limits:
      cpu: "1"
      memory: 1Gi


controller:
  replicas: 1


applicationSet:
  replicas: 2


notifications:
  enabled: true



